WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
*Film rolling*

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:10.040
*Old cinematic music and countdown beeps*

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.380
Let me start by saying there is no greatest movie of all time,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.100
The question is just too difficult to really answer.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.980
But I still think it's the most important question in cinema.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.860
There are a number of concrete aspects of a movie that

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.400
That turn it from good movies to movies with a legacy.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.120
The first is cinematic influence,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.680
How did the movie change the way movies are made?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.440
If you read anything about Citizen Kane,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.600
You'll realise that it's probably the most innovative movie of all time

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.700
And that's a big reason why it tops every all time movie list.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.900
Citizen Kane highlights a very clear distinction between two eras of film making,

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.780
Especially in it's cinematography.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.380
Silent films were very economical with their editing,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.200
They either showed their actors on a simple two-dimensional plane,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.940
Or used montage cuts to quickly associate one idea with another.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.400
And once sound was introduced in the late 1920's,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.640
The first instinct for many directors was just filming dialog

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.780
shot reverse shot with shallow focus.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.720
And it was boring.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.500
"Here is your prize, Sir Archer."

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.960
"It is indeed an honour to receive it from the hands of so beautiful a lady."

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.940
"How is it that you didn't use a black arrow today?"

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.240
"That is my court of last resort, Sir Guy."

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.620
But Orson Welles and Gregg Toland changed the game in 1941,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.060
By bringing back traditional theatrical staging on to the screen.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.360
The use of deep focused camera allowed characters to be staged

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.840
In interesting and revealing ways.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.700
"Kane, you are the soul owner."

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.180
Showing power dynamics, transitions of power, complex visual story telling,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.040
And giving the viewer active participation in the scene.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.140
Allowing the viewer to choose what to look at,

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.700
Instead of having a close up forcing you to watch one character at a time.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.040
Welles and Toland weren't the first to use this technique,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.940
But they executed it so effectively that they started a movement.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.080
The iconic deep focus and sharp lighting of film noir movies?

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.980
All influenced from Citizen Kane.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.220
Not to mention Welles' use of sound to transition from one scene to another,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.280
Which is basically in every single movie now.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.400
Not to mention the makeup, which had never been done so extensively.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.560
Not to mention it's extensive use of flashback and unreliable narration,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.660
Which had only been done majorly in literature.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.100
Once you think about it, there's no question of it's greatness.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.540
Great movies change things,

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.460
Star Wars ended a streak of dreary and sophisticated movies in the 1970's,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.300
The Blair Witch Project changed the horror genre for good,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.700
And it looks like Deadpool's gonna change the game when it comes to marketing a movie,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.840
And we'll probably see a lot more R-rated comedies in the future because of it.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.060
If a movie changed what other film makers were doing, it's gotta be at least a little bit great.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.580
The second measure of greatness is the cultural impact.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.620
How did the movie change the actual world it was made in, not just the world of cinema?

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.940
Like when Jaws made beach attendance plummet,

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:38.360
Or when Fight Club made actual fight clubs spring up around the US.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.660
Personally, I think if the movie Fight Club inspired you to start your own fight club

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.600
The you're totally misreading the movie, but that's a topic for another time.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.480
All I'm saying is movies aren't in a bubble.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.560
Movies can make laws get enacted, they can spread awareness on a massive scale to billions of people.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.220
The ones that catch the attention of enough people are great,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.120
Even if it's just bringing back folk music.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.580
"#The place where he, was born and raised."

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.840
The third and final way is personal impact, it's the way the movie changes you.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.300
I think this is the most overlooked aspect of a great movie because it's hard to measure,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.080
But I've always been a big believer that movies can tell us more about how to live our lives than the real world does.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.820
I think Juno explores abortion in a more accessible way than any abortion pamphlet,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.040
Or any talk show host.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.880
"Yeah! And she was like 'Oh hi, babies have fingernails.' Fingernails!"

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.040
And I think that ability to tap into reality through a story

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.580
makes a movie one of the greats,

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.880
And it's the underlying beauty of cinema.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.840
A movie doesn't have to change cinema forever,

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.880
And it doesn't have to change the world forever,

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.200
But if it changes you forever, it's gotta be pretty great.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.720
I know this videos going more sentimental than usual,

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.820
But greatness is hard to discuss otherwise.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.060
There's certainly measurable cinematic techniques to judge for greatness,

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.100
But at the end of the day, it's the way movies touch us personally that makes them great.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.440
People pick different movies as their favourite, because at that point in their life

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.740
that was the movie they had to watch to help them out,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.460
Or just to astound them the most.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.780
It could be as simple as a movie that makes you laugh a lot.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.700
I find that if I really liked the movie, I think about it constantly after I've seen it.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.620
500 Days Of Summer really helped me with a bad relationship because

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.600
it felt like the movie was talking about me,

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.680
And explaining everything I was currently feeling so perfectly.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.780
"I HATE THIS SONG!"

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.720
So how about this.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.860
I'm normally terrible at keeping up with social media,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.280
But tweet about you favourite movie to my Twitter @SwagThug4Lyfe

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.280
And I'll respond to it.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:28.780
Let's talk about great movies.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.860
And while I did start by saying there is no greatest movie of all time,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.360
It's definitely The Princess Bride.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.040
"LIAR!"

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.680
"LIAR!"

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:36.660
"Get back witch!"

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.000
"I'M NOT A WITCH I'M YOUR WIFE!"

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:38.760
Thanks for watching.

